.TH HASHMAP_GET_ENTRY 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
hashmap_get_entry \- Retrieve an entry from a hashmap using a specified key length

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/hashmap.h>

.IB hashentry*
.IB hashmap_get_entry(hashmap* map, const char* key, size_t keylen);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBhashmap_get_entry\fR function retrieves an entry associated with a key from a hashmap. It takes a pointer to the hashmap, the key, and the length of the key as arguments.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB hashmap*
A pointer to the hashmap.
.IB const char*
The key whose entry is to be retrieved.
.IB size_t
The length of the key.

The function returns a pointer to the hashentry associated with the key, or NULL if the key is not found in the hashmap.

The \fBhashmap_get_entry\fR function is intended to be used to access entries in a hashmap when the key length is known.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBhashmap_get_entry\fR function to retrieve an entry from a hashmap using a specified key length:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/hashmap.h>

int main() {
    hashmap* map = hashmap_new();
    const char* key = "example";

    hashmap_set(map, key, "value");

    hashentry* entry = hashmap_get_entry(map, key, 7);

    if (entry) {
        printf("Key: %s\n", entry->key);
        printf("Value: %s\n", (char*)entry->val);
    } else {
        printf("Key not found\n");
    }

    hashmap_free(map);

    return 0;
}
.nef